From 012b0605a5c34b750f8dce40d192d7b53b38a7ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 10:10:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 089/119] scene-referred S6: the capture commands, and a frame
 that says what took it

rec_CaptureEXR <folder> [prefix] arms a sequence, rec_CaptureEXRStop ends it, rec_CaptureEXRFrame
writes exactly one. Commands and not a cvar, because arming takes arguments and because
capture_frames has already demonstrated what happens when a capture's on/off state and its frame
counter share one console variable that ManualFrameStep and CryMovie also write. This counter
belongs to the capture and starts at zero on every arm. Files are <prefix>.%06d.exr, dot-separated,
which is what Nuke and Resolve auto-detect as a sequence rather than as a folder of stills.

Four cvars decide what the file IS, all latched when the capture starts: the tap (graded or
negative), the encoding, the compression, and the fixed step. The encoding default is ACES2065-1 -
AP0, converted from AP1 during the write, tagged with AP0 chromaticities AND acesImageContainerFlag
- because an untagged EXR in an ACES project is assumed to be ACES2065-1, so the file imports
correctly with the operator doing nothing at all. ACEScg is the alternative: the buffer byte for
byte, AP1 chromaticities and never the container flag, since that flag asserts ST 2065-4
conformance and that is a claim about AP0 data.

A sequence sets t_FixedStep and puts it back on stop. Outside the editor nothing else locks frame
time - capture_frames certainly does not, whatever its name suggests - so without this a sequence
is one file per rendered frame at whatever rate the machine managed, and N frames is not N/fps
seconds. An EXR sequence is a timed artefact or it cannot be conformed. It also pins
r_DepthOfFieldLensModelJitter to 2 for the duration: a sequence in which every frame carries a
different sub-pixel dither is noise with a picture behind it, and it is indistinguishable from TAA
ghosting when someone tries to work out what went wrong.

Metadata reaches the renderer on the post-effect bus, which is already the one channel the camera
and the renderer share - cheaper than a new CryCommon interface for a string. The bus gains a
string param type for the two values that are genuinely names, and ccam publishes camera name,
focal length, T-stop (not f-number: the T-stop is what the exposure is computed from, and
`aperture` is the classic attribute name to get wrong), ISO, shutter, ND and EV100 every frame it
owns the render state. The renderer adds the exposure scale actually applied, the working space,
the tap, the ODT and LMT names (read off the bound LUT textures), the CDL in the ASC's own order,
the frame index and the render frame ID.

Two of those are load-bearing rather than decorative. rec/exposureScale is the number that makes
the file's absolute scale recoverable at all - this whole pipeline rests on 1.0 = 10 000 cd/m2 and
the pre-exposure destroys that unless the scale travels with the frame. rec/exportTap is what lets
anything reading these files tell a graded frame from a negative, which look alike and mean
entirely different things.

rec/lensName is published empty on purpose: lens presets are a later item, and an invented name
stamped on every frame would be worse than no name at all.

